==============================================================================
1. Third-party audio inside the released clips
==============================================================================

One development clip (item vxp-carsvc-0001, variant on_shoulder) mixes the
caller's speech over a real traffic recording:

  DEMAND: J. Thiemann, N. Ito, E. Vincent, "DEMAND: a collection of
  multi-channel recordings of acoustic noise in diverse environments" (scene
  STRAFFIC, channel ch01, a 60 s excerpt from 165 s), Zenodo,
  https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1227121. Licensed CC BY 4.0.

  Licence note: the Zenodo record's licence field is CC BY 4.0, while the
  DEMAND.pdf inside the record states CC BY-SA 3.0. We treated the record-level
  CC BY 4.0 as governing. The rebuild recipe (download URL, SHA-256 of the
  original and of the converted excerpt, conversion command) is
  stimuli/packs/realbeds/traffic.json. The recording itself is not
  redistributed separately.

All other scene sounds in the development split are generated by our own code
(white noise, line drop, radio squelch, eCall modem tones) or rendered by the
same text-to-speech engine as the caller (a second, prompting voice; a TV
advertisement).

==============================================================================
2. Synthetic speech: Google Gemini text-to-speech
==============================================================================

Every released clip is synthetic speech rendered with Google Gemini TTS
(gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview and gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts; voices Kore,
Leda and Gacrux), including the child voices. No clip is a human recording and
no voice was cloned. Each manifest row names its engine, model and voice.

- Ownership (PX-043). The Gemini API Additional Terms state that Google does not
  claim ownership of generated content. We read ourselves as the owner who
  permits redistribution and release the clips under CC BY 4.0. Google's
  Prohibited Use Policy bars presenting generated content as made solely by a
  human: every clip is labelled synthetic. This is our reading of the terms,
  not a ruling. Terms checked at https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms
  (last verified: see licence_basis.verified in each manifest row).
- Unpaid tier (PX-003). Most clips were rendered on the Gemini API's unpaid
  tier, whose terms allow Google to use submitted content and generated
  responses to improve its products. The item text, style prompts and audio of
  the development split, and the cue-judge prompts, may therefore have been
  exposed to Google's training pipelines. Results for Google models should be
  read with this in mind.

==============================================================================
3. Automatic speech recognition used to admit clips (PX-010)
==============================================================================

When the bank was frozen, 79 of the 81 development clips were admitted by an
ASR round-trip check that used Deepgram Nova-3, and 7 slot-noise clips had the
masked span placed using Deepgram word timings. Deepgram's terms restrict
benchmarking, so no Deepgram output is released: its transcripts and
slot-alignment strings are stripped from the manifest, which keeps only the
verdict and word error rate. Every clip was re-checked with a local
Whisper-large-v3-turbo model (gates.asr_regate_local); the admission decision
is unchanged for all 81. Details: docs/release/tables/deepgram_provenance.md.

==============================================================================
4. Model outputs in the per-cell run records
==============================================================================

The run records contain outputs of the systems evaluated in the paper. Their
providers' terms apply to those outputs as follows.

- Derived fields only (PX-031, PX-037, PX-041/042). Rows from xAI Grok Voice,
  from NVIDIA nemotron-3-nano-omni served on the OpenRouter ":free" tier, and
  from MiMo requests that OpenRouter routed to the StreamLake upstream carry
  scores, chosen tool names and metrics only; response text, tool arguments and
  transcripts are removed (field "restricted"). NVIDIA's trial terms also
  allow NVIDIA to collect submitted content for model improvement, so the
  development items and tool menus were exposed to it.
- OpenRouter (PX-039). Many systems were reached through OpenRouter, which
  routes each request to an upstream host. The upstream is recorded per row in
  metrics.upstream_provider; routing was not pinned unless noted. OpenRouter
  states that it does not train on inputs or outputs; each upstream's own terms
  bind the outputs.
- Xiaomi MiMo (PX-033). Xiaomi's platform terms do not state whether inputs are
  used for training.
- Ultravox v0.5 (PX-046). Built with Llama. Llama 3.1 is licensed under the
  Llama 3.1 Community License, Copyright (c) Meta Platforms, Inc. All Rights
  Reserved. Ultravox is used as an instrument, not a contestant.
- NemotronLabs VoiceChat 11B (PX-053). Weights under the OpenMDW-1.1 licence;
  results are for a locally run 4-bit quantized copy, not the reference
  weights.
- SenseVoiceSmall and emotion2vec_plus_large (PX-050, PX-004). The acoustic-tag
  cascade uses SenseVoiceSmall (FunAudioLLM) and emotion2vec_plus_large
  (FunASR), both under the FunASR Model License v1.1 (as of September 2026),
  which covers the model weights and their derivatives, not inference outputs.
  Their tags appear in that cascade's records.

Every model output is machine-generated and is labelled with the system that
produced it (the record's driver field).

